  "summary": "I wrote src/main.ts and it passes typecheck. I couldn't run it in a browser, so I haven't seen a rendered frame. Light intensities and material values are untested guesses.\n\n- **Floor:** a generated seamless sweep with a flat floor, a curved section and a vertical backdrop. It is light grey and receives shadows.\n- **Spheres:** three spheres of equal size in a row.\n  - Left is `material.clearGlass`, with transmission 1, IOR 1.5 and a faint tint.\n  - Centre is `material.brushedMetal`: fully metallic, anisotropic, with a procedural brushed normal map.\n  - Right is dark red `material.fabric`, with roughness 1 and no metalness. It has a strong sheen and a procedural weave normal map.\n- **Lighting:**\n  - A drifting key directional light casts soft (PCSS) shadows.\n  - A dimmer fill comes from the opposite side, and a rim light from behind.\n  - A neutral environment probe gives the metal and glass something to reflect.\n  - A hemisphere light, contact shadows and mild bloom complete the setup.\n- **Key light drift:** its X position moves on a slow sine, `-1.2 + 3.6·sin(0.45t)`, and is updated every frame through `app.nodes`.\n- **Camera:** fixed perspective camera, slightly raised and three-quarter front, with all three spheres centred in frame.\n- **Reflections:** the glass reflects the environment probe, not the key, fill and rim lights themselves. Those lights only give it specular highlights.\n- **Fabric weave:** the weave is a procedural normal map only. I did not add a base-colour weave texture, so it may be hard to see at 640x360.\n- **State and ready flag:** `window.__TASK_STATE__` is set before the first frame and refreshed every frame with the three sphere names and the current `keyLightX`. `window.__EVAL_READY__` is set after `app.ready()`.\n\n",
